# P, THE OPERATION INDEX, AS A RELATION OF ITS OWN. One row per operation a filing declares.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE OTHER CONFORMED DIMENSION, AND IT OWES A FILE FOR THE SAME REASON `layer` DOES.
#    `draw` and `induction` are both P x L fact tables, so a question about operations asked
#    through an ENTRY table gets the operations that happen to have entries rather than the
#    operations that EXIST. ⚠️ The two coincide only because `pm:Operation` requires at least one
#    draw or induction, which is a fact about the grammar that no query states.
#
# ⛔ A DIMENSION WITHOUT A FILE CANNOT BE A LEFT OPERAND, so no law in algebra/ could ever have
#   governed a count of operations, and diagrams/roster.sqlc's `tasks` law would have had nothing
#   to count against. Same repair as composition/carriable.sqlc and
#   composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc: the difference needs a named left operand.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND P CARRIES THE CROSSING, BECAUSE THE CROSSING IS AN ATTRIBUTE OF AN OPERATION AND
#    NOT AN ENTRY OF ANYTHING. `pm:Operation/foreignId` is at most one per operation, so it
#    sits on the dimension the way `part_regime` sits on a part. `entries/notation_references.sqlc`
#    is the restriction to the operations that file one, and it is a σ over this rather than a
#    second read of `pm.operation`, so the two cannot drift about what an operation is.
-- pm:Operation, keyed (filing, label), with the notation position or the reason there is none.
-- ⚠️ `foreign_absent` AS TEXT, for `diagrams/searches.sqlc`'s reason: an emitter reads this and
--    a Postgres enum has no built-in mapping on the Rust side. The type still guards the INSERT,
--    which is where a wrong word has to be caught. `epistemics/absences.sqlc` casts it back.
SELECT o.filing, o.label, o.foreign_notation, o.foreign_id, o.foreign_absent::text AS foreign_absent
FROM pm.operation o
